practiceProblems.description:
When you know exactly how many times you want to loop through a block of code, use the '**for**' loop instead of a '**while**' loop:

    Syntax = for variable in expression {
            // code block to be executed
            }

The 'variable' takes on the value of each element in 'expression', which can be a range or any collection type (like arrays, vectors, etc.), and the 'code' is executed for each of these values.

    Example
            for i in 0..5 {
               println!("{}", i);
            }

The code above does the following:
- Defines a range from 0 to 5 (exclusive of 5)
- For each value in this range, it outputs the value of i to the console.

### Task
Write a program which does the following
- Initialise a variable $n$ and accept a user defined input from the console
- Output "Rust" to the console $n$ number of times.

